# Cloudflare Workers Development Guide

Version: 1.0.0

Comprehensive guide for developing with Cloudflare Workers, Wrangler CLI, and related services in this project.

## Wrangler Configuration

### [wrangler.json](mdc:wrangler.json) Structure

The main configuration file for Cloudflare Workers:

```jsonc
{
  "name": "agent-chat",
  "main": "./src/worker/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-04-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "nodejs_compat_populate_process_env"],
  "observability": { "enabled": true },
  "upload_source_maps": true,
  "assets": { "not_found_handling": "single-page-application" }
}
```

### Environment Configuration

Two environments configured: `dev` and `prod`

**Development Environment:**
```jsonc
"env": {
  "dev": {
    "vars": { "CLOUDFLARE_ENV": "dev" },
    "durable_objects": {
      "bindings": [
        { "name": "Chat", "class_name": "Chat" },
        { "name": "ChatAgent", "class_name": "ChatAgent" }
      ]
    },
    "d1_databases": [
      {
        "binding": "DB",
        "database_name": "my-db-name",
        "database_id": "9cc06525-7e6f-4b34-a1f7-3a055337b9fb",
        "migrations_dir": "./src/worker/drizzle"
      }
    ],
    "ai": { "binding": "AI" }
  }
}
```

## Wrangler CLI Commands

### Development Commands

```bash
# Start local development server
wrangler dev

# Start with specific environment
CLOUDFLARE_ENV=dev wrangler dev

# Build and check bundle size
wrangler deploy --dry-run
```

### Deployment Commands

```bash
# Deploy to development
npm run deploy

# Deploy to production
npm run deploy:prod

# Build without deploying
npm run build
npm run build:prod
```

### Database Commands

```bash
# Generate migrations from schema
npm run db:generate

# Apply migrations locally
npm run db:migrate-local

# Apply migrations to dev environment
npm run db:migrate:dev

# Apply migrations to production
npm run db:migrate:prod

# Open database studio
npm run db:studio
```

### Type Generation

```bash
# Generate types for dev environment
npm run cf-typegen

# Generate types for prod environment
npm run cf-typegen:prod
```

## Worker Entry Point

### [src/worker/index.ts](mdc:src/worker/index.ts)

Main worker file structure:

```typescript
export { Chat } from "./agents/chat";
export { ChatAgent } from "./agents/multi-model-agent";
import { trpcServer } from "@hono/trpc-server";
import { logger } from "hono/logger"
import { Hono } from "hono";
import { createContext } from "./trpc";
import { appRouter } from "./routes";
import { routeAgentRequest } from "agents";
import { auth } from "./lib/auth";

const app = new Hono<{ Bindings: Env }>();

// Middleware
app.use("*", logger());

// Authentication routes
app.on(["POST", "GET"], "/api/auth/**", (c) => auth.handler(c.req.raw));

// Health check
app.get("/api/health", (c) => c.json({ status: true }, 200))

// tRPC routes
app.use("/trpc/*", async (c, next) =>
    trpcServer({
        router: appRouter,
        createContext: () => createContext(c),
    })(c, next)
);

// Agent routes
app.get("/agents/**", async (c) => {
    return (await routeAgentRequest(c.req.raw, c.env)) || new Response("Not found", { status: 404 })
})

export default app;
```

## Bindings Configuration

### Environment Variables (Env interface)

Automatically generated in `worker-configuration.d.ts`:

```typescript
interface Env {
  // Database binding
  DB: D1Database;
  
  // AI model binding
  AI: Ai;
  
  // Durable Object bindings
  Chat: DurableObjectNamespace;
  ChatAgent: DurableObjectNamespace;
  
  // Environment variables
  CLOUDFLARE_ENV: string;
  
  // Add your environment variables here
  OPENAI_API_KEY?: string;
  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY?: string;
}
```

### Durable Objects

Agents are implemented as Durable Objects with persistence:

```typescript
// In wrangler.json
"durable_objects": {
  "bindings": [
    { "name": "Chat", "class_name": "Chat" },
    { "name": "ChatAgent", "class_name": "ChatAgent" }
  ]
},
"migrations": [
  {
    "tag": "v0",
    "new_sqlite_classes": ["Chat", "ChatAgent"]
  }
]
```

### D1 Database

SQLite database binding configuration:

```typescript
// Accessing the database in your worker
export default {
  async fetch(request, env, ctx) {
    // Query the database
    const result = await env.DB.prepare("SELECT * FROM users").all();
    return Response.json(result);
  }
}
```

### AI Binding

Access to Cloudflare AI models:

```typescript
// Using the AI binding
export default {
  async fetch(request, env, ctx) {
    const response = await env.AI.run("@cf/meta/llama-2-7b-chat-int8", {
      messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Hello!" }]
    });
    return Response.json(response);
  }
}
```

## Development Best Practices

### 1. Environment Variables

Store sensitive data in environment files:
- `.dev.vars.dev` - Development secrets
- `.dev.vars.prod` - Production secrets

Never commit these files to version control.

### 2. Type Safety

Generate types after configuration changes:
```bash
npm run cf-typegen
```

This creates type definitions for your bindings in `worker-configuration.d.ts`.

### 3. Local Development

```bash
# Start development with hot reloading
npm run dev

# This runs both:
# - Vite dev server for React app
# - Wrangler dev for Workers backend
```

### 4. Error Handling

Use proper error handling in your workers:

```typescript
export default {
  async fetch(request, env, ctx) {
    try {
      // Your worker logic
      return new Response("Success");
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("Worker error:", error);
      return new Response("Internal Server Error", { status: 500 });
    }
  }
}
```

### 5. Logging

Use structured logging for debugging:

```typescript
import { logger } from "hono/logger";

const app = new Hono();
app.use("*", logger());

// In your handlers
console.log("Processing request:", { url: request.url, method: request.method });
```

## Deployment Workflow

### 1. Development Deployment

```bash
npm run deploy
```

This:
- Builds the project with `CLOUDFLARE_ENV=dev`
- Deploys to the dev environment
- Uses dev database and environment variables

### 2. Production Deployment

```bash
npm run deploy:prod
```

This:
- Builds the project with `CLOUDFLARE_ENV=prod`
- Deploys to the production environment
- Uses production database and environment variables

### 3. Database Migrations

Always run migrations after deployment:

```bash
# For development
npm run db:migrate:dev

# For production
npm run db:migrate:prod
```

## Monitoring and Debugging

### 1. Real-time Logs

View logs in development:
```bash
wrangler tail
```

### 2. Analytics

Access Worker analytics in the Cloudflare dashboard to monitor:
- Request volume
- Error rates
- Response times
- CPU usage

### 3. Observability

Observability is enabled in `wrangler.json`:
```jsonc
"observability": { "enabled": true }
```

This provides detailed metrics and tracing.

## Common Patterns

### 1. Request Routing

```typescript
const app = new Hono<{ Bindings: Env }>();

app.get("/api/users", async (c) => {
  const users = await c.env.DB.prepare("SELECT * FROM users").all();
  return c.json(users.results);
});

app.post("/api/users", async (c) => {
  const body = await c.req.json();
  // Insert user logic
  return c.json({ success: true });
});
```

### 2. Middleware Usage

```typescript
import { cors } from 'hono/cors';
import { logger } from 'hono/logger';

app.use("*", logger());
app.use("*", cors({
  origin: ["http://localhost:5173", "https://your-domain.com"],
  credentials: true,
}));
```

### 3. Error Boundaries

```typescript
app.onError((err, c) => {
  console.error("Unhandled error:", err);
  return c.json({ error: "Internal Server Error" }, 500);
});
```
